/g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> को उपयुक्त लिगैंड बनाते हैं। इसलिए, कथन I सही है।</p>\n\n<p>कथन II: यद्यपि N (नाइट्रोजन) और P (फॉस्फोरस) दोनों आवर्त सारणी के समूह 15 से हैं और कुछ समान गुण साझा करते हैं, संक्रमण धातुओं के साथ उनके बंधन की प्रकृति हमेशा समान नहीं होती है। नाइट्रोजन यौगिक आमतौर पर फॉस्फोरस यौगिकों की तुलना में धातुओं के साथ अधिक मजबूत बंध बनाते हैं। यह अंतर कई कारकों के कारण होता है, जिनमें परमाणु आकार में अंतर, विद्युतऋणात्मकता और कक्षीय अतिव्यापन की सीमा शामिल है। नाइट्रोजन, छोटा और अधिक विद्युतऋणी होने के कारण, फॉस्फोरस की तुलना में धातुओं के साथ अधिक प्रभावी अतिव्यापन और मजबूत बंध बना सकता है। इसलिए, कथन II गलत है।</p>\n\n<p>इस विश्लेषण को देखते हुए, सबसे उपयुक्त उत्तर विकल्प D है: कथन I सही है लेकिन कथन II गलत है।</p>"}},"subject":"chemistry","subjectGroup":null,"timeAllotted":120000,"topic":"application-of-coordination-compound-and-organometallic-compounds","type":"mcq","year":2024,"isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"tags":["assertion_reason"],"yearKey":"jee-main-2024-online-8th-april-morning-shift","active":true,"options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.\n"},{"identifier":"B","content":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.\n"},{"identifier":"C","content":"Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.\n"},{"identifier":"D","content":"Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."}],"correct_options":["D"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Option D - Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n\n<p>Statement I: <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.681ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.763ex\" height=\"2.378ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 3873.1 1050.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"4E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4E\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(750,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"43\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-43\"></use><use data-c=\"48\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-48\" transform=\"translate(722,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1505,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2297.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2719.6,-285.4) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> (trimethylamine) and <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.681ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.607ex\" height=\"2.378ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 3804.1 1050.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"50\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-50\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(681,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"43\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-43\"></use><use data-c=\"48\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-48\" transform=\"translate(722,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1505,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2297.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2719.6,-285.4) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> (trimethylphosphine) can indeed act as ligands to form transition metal complexes. Ligands are molecules or ions that can donate a pair of electrons to a metal atom to form a coordinate bond. Both nitrogen and phosphorus have lone pairs of electrons that can be donated to transition metals, making <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.681ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.763ex\" height=\"2.378ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 3873.1 1050.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"4E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4E\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(750,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"43\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-43\"></use><use data-c=\"48\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-48\" transform=\"translate(722,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1505,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2297.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2719.6,-285.4) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> and <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.681ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.607ex\" height=\"2.378ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 3804.1 1050.9\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"50\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-50\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(681,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mrow\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"28\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-28\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(389,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"43\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-43\"></use><use data-c=\"48\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-48\" transform=\"translate(722,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1505,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2297.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2719.6,-285.4) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> suitable ligands. Therefore, Statement I is correct.</p>\n\n<p>Statement II: While N (Nitrogen) and P (Phosphorus) are both from group 15 of the periodic table and share some similar properties, the nature of their bonding with transition metals is not always the same. Nitrogen compounds typically form stronger bonds with metals compared to phosphorus compounds. This difference is due to several factors, including the difference in atomic sizes, electronegativity, and the extent of orbital overlap. Nitrogen, being smaller and more electronegative, can form more effective overlap and stronger bonds with metals compared to phosphorus. Therefore, Statement II is incorrect.</p>\n\n<p>Given this analysis, the most appropriate answer is Option D: Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.</p>"}]}